WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.430
In this video I’ll be analysing how horror
2018 stacks up with other years for the genre.

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:09.080
If you want to hear what might be in store
for 2019, then stick around to the end of

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:09.940
this video.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:17.391
An article has been making the rounds in the
horror community titled, ‘Wasn’t This

00:00:17.391 --> 00:00:19.480
Supposed To Be The Golden Age Of Horror?’

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:23.270
The author’s thesis is that the horror genre
has really deflated since last year and there

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:25.820
hasn’t been a good scary movie in 2018.

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:29.820
Now last time I talked about an article on
DeadTalks, I basically destroyed the guy,

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:33.720
to the point that he completely went silent
on the matter and deleted all his tweets linking

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:34.720
to the article.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:38.730
My goal this time is not to destroy anyone,
but to simply to look back and see how this

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:43.390
year stacks up to previous years in terms
of what audiences thought, what critics thought,

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:44.390
and what I thought.

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:49.080
At least, that was my initial goal, to offer
an objective comparison of each year.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:53.500
My hypothesis was that 2018 isn’t really
a bad year overall, but compared to the amazing

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:58.430
years we had in 2016 and even to some extent
last year in 2017, 2018 just doesn’t have

00:00:58.430 --> 00:00:59.720
the firepower to compete.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:03.360
However, as I started to crunch the numbers,
I realized more and more issues with this

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:04.360
comparison.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:08.610
So my new point of this video, is that you
can’t really objectively compare year to

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:09.610
year.

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:12.780
I can give my own feelings on which years
were best, but even those are going to be

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:14.939
skewed and here’s why.

00:01:14.939 --> 00:01:19.280
The first year I started logging every film
I watched was 2014, so let’s start there.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:23.030
This is where I realized the first issue with
this experiment -- how do you define a horror

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:24.030
film?

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:27.420
If you include dark thrillers like Gone Girl
or Nightcrawler, then I’d consider 2014

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:28.979
to be a pretty decent year.

00:01:28.979 --> 00:01:33.520
If you take those out, you’ve got It Follows
and The Guest, both of which I love, but then

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:36.179
after that, there aren’t a lot of heavy
hitters.

00:01:36.179 --> 00:01:39.710
Even if you only go by horror movies by the
strictest definition of the word, there are

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:42.150
basically countless amounts of movies that
come out every year.

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:45.060
Do you include all MPAA major releases?

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.170
But then what about indie films?

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:51.520
Do you try to include good, bad and mid-tier
films from each year and take the average

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:53.630
audience, critic and personal scores of each
movie?

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:57.020
What happens if you have a bunch of good films,
but also a bunch of bad ones?

00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:59.189
Is that considered a good year or a bad year?

00:01:59.189 --> 00:02:02.520
What if you have the best film of the decade
one year, but it’s weighed down some stinkers

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:03.990
that also came out that year?

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:06.649
How do you even decide what year a movie came
out?

00:02:06.649 --> 00:02:10.300
Many films premiere at a festival, then have
their wide release a different year, so the

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:13.720
year that the majority of people watch the
movie is not the year the movie came out?

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:17.500
Let’s not even start to bring up the fact
that the article claims 2018 was a bad year

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:22.060
for horror, yet it only talks about movies
with the addition of a couple TV shows and

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:23.670
high budget web-series.

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:27.580
For simplicity’s sake, we can’t even get
into games or books… and I know you’re

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:30.840
not going to diss on horror YouTubers, otherwise
we’re gonna have a PROBLEM!!!

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:34.980
But yeah, I really don’t see a fair way
to compare these years.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:38.510
You could frame any of them to be the worst
year of the bunch, maybe with the exception

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:39.510
of 2016.

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:42.840
I don’t see how anyone could come to the
conclusion that that year sucked, but then

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:46.310
again, I’m just basing that off of my opinion
of the movies that I saw.

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:50.350
I try to see movies that I think I’m gonna
like, but I imagine a different person with

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:54.320
different tastes, could see a different set
of movies and have different opinions of each

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:55.320
movie.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:59.090
See... this is why I analyse films in Things
You Missed and I’m not a ranker, or reviewer.

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:02.740
But with that all being said, let’s get
to what you’re all here, for: my opinion.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:06.400
So we start with 2014.

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:10.720
I’ll be going by the movie release dates
listed on a film database called TMDB.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:13.530
It uses the original premiere dates of the
film as it’s release date.

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:17.250
So although I didn’t actually get to see
It Follows until 2015, when it was released

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:21.980
commercially in theaters, it will technically
be considered a 2014 film for the purposes

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:22.980
of this argument.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:27.140
I can already tell you It Follows was my favorite
film of the decade, and really, my favorite

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:28.870
film in a long time.

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:32.510
The other highlights are, as I mentioned,
probably closer to the thriller side of the

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:37.360
venn diagram: Gone Girl and Nightcrawler,
before horror films like The Guest and [REC]

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:38.360
4.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:39.630
And let’s get this out of the way now -- The
Babadook.

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:44.450
I know people love that movie and that’s
fine, I found it to be kind of an average

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:48.870
cliche supernatural movie that came out basically
at the end of the supernatural trend that

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:51.520
Paranormal Activity started in 2007.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:55.840
The following year was 2015, and that’s
also pretty solid, headlined by Joel Edgerton’s

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:57.380
surprise hit The Gift.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:00.720
This is where we also have to address the
question of what we do with horror-comedy,

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:05.300
and since The Visit is equal parts horror
and comedy, I’m willing to put that on the

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:06.300
list.

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:09.630
Insidious: Chapter 3 was almost a skip for
me, but I am so glad I ended up seeing it,

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:11.870
because it might just be as good as the first
Insidious.

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:14.650
I’d say 2015 has a few drawbacks though.

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:18.519
Unfriended was at least funny in an unintentional
way...

00:04:18.519 --> 00:04:19.519
"Ken?

00:04:19.519 --> 00:04:20.519
Ken?"

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:21.440
"What is he doing?"

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:22.440
"Ken!!"

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:26.200
"Oh man, that's hilarious"
"Is he OK?"

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:27.260
"Ken?"

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:28.380
"HAHA!

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:29.320
Oh my God!

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:30.480
WAHAHA!

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:32.120
He put his hand in the blender!

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:34.160
WAHAHAHAHA!

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:35.760
You gotta be kidding me!

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:40.120
That's how he kills himself?"

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:45.600
...but you may have forgotten about The
Poltergeist remake and the even more maligned

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:47.920
Paranormal Activity: The Ghost Dimension.

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:51.550
I liked both of these years, but even the
strength of their highlights isn’t going

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:53.330
to stack up to what would follow.

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:59.120
Of my top in 2016, 6 of them are in the territory
of horror or dark thriller.

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:03.180
If you saw my last video you already know
how I feel about Split, but add on 10 Cloverfield

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:07.401
Lane, Don’t Breathe, Light’s Out, the
arguably more thriller oriented Nerve, but

00:05:07.401 --> 00:05:11.680
also the more traditional horror piece, The
Boy and you’ve got a stellar list of highlights.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:16.650
But even 2016 isn’t without a few disappointments,
and they came from respectable directors,

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:21.139
like Adam Wingard's’ Blair Witch, Rob Zombie’s
31 and Gore Verbinski’s return to horror

00:05:21.139 --> 00:05:22.600
with A Cure for Wellness.

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:27.270
The author of the article seemed to have a
lot of praise for 2017, so let’s look at

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:28.590
what we got that year.

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:31.860
It Comes At Night was the leader of the pack
for me, I’ll never understand why people

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:36.020
thought the trailer was misleading or something
-- or how that would make the movie itself

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:40.029
lose credibility, but that might be a topic
for a different DeadTalk.

00:05:40.029 --> 00:05:43.740
I will say that It Comes At Night doesn’t
have the punch that It Follows had in ‘14,

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:48.650
The Gift had in ‘15 or Split had in ‘16,
but you can’t really blame Shultsy for that,

00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:50.199
it’s still very well done.

00:05:50.199 --> 00:05:54.371
‘17 might be the year of divisiveness actually,
because I’ve got mother! near the top of

00:05:54.371 --> 00:05:55.620
my list as well.

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:56.779
And who could forget IT?

00:05:56.779 --> 00:06:01.870
I know I included The Visit, but I’m not
going to include Get Out, since that one is

00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:04.270
much closer to comedy than it is horror.

00:06:04.270 --> 00:06:07.539
This next one I’m only going to point out
because I know people will pull out some less

00:06:07.539 --> 00:06:14.249
than desirable films when we get to the 2018
discussion.

00:06:14.249 --> 00:06:21.279
Let’s not forget that one of the worst atrocities
of the decade took place in 2017.

00:06:21.279 --> 00:06:23.610
"Please don't say it."

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:27.349
At least Paranormal Activity: The Ghost Dimension
was the 6th movie of a franchise that had

00:06:27.349 --> 00:06:29.449
already lost it’s touch several movies ago.

00:06:29.449 --> 00:06:32.559
The Bye Bye Man really has no excuses for
itself.

00:06:32.559 --> 00:06:35.340
In fact, I saw this movie before it officially
came out.

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:39.560
This was before it had achieved meme status,
and all I could come up to say about this

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:44.729
film was, “a movie so bad, I can pretty
much guarantee it will become a meme.”

00:06:44.729 --> 00:06:47.499
I literally predicted the future when I wrote
that.

00:06:47.499 --> 00:06:49.139
Let’s look at some of the popular reviews.

00:06:49.139 --> 00:06:51.949
The peepee poopoo man.

00:06:51.949 --> 00:06:53.629
457 likes.

00:06:53.629 --> 00:06:57.289
I’ve been chanting ‘the bye bye man, the
bye bye man’ for the past 2 hours to see

00:06:57.289 --> 00:07:00.050
if i can get him to kill me so i can forget
about this movie.

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:04.289
I’m sorry I really don’t like focusing
on the negative, but there’s just a special

00:07:04.289 --> 00:07:05.710
place in hell for this one.

00:07:05.710 --> 00:07:07.800
So that just leaves us with 2018.

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:12.530
I feel like most of us will agree that Hereditary
and A Quiet Place should occupy the top two

00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:14.939
spots so far this year in some order.

00:07:14.939 --> 00:07:19.520
Insidious: The Last Key wasn’t terrible
for a January film, and for a fourth film

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:22.839
in a franchise on it’s third director…
it was acceptable.

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:27.360
The rest of the year… it had people literally
eating Tide Pods.

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:32.280
We had The Cloverfield Paradox -- not the
worst thing I’ve ever seen, and kind of

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:36.300
goes more sci-fi than horror, but when you
put it next to the first two movies, it just

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:37.300
pales.

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:39.969
And who could forget this historic Things
You Missed intro?

00:07:39.969 --> 00:07:46.629
Roll it.

00:07:46.629 --> 00:07:53.099
(Clip)

00:07:53.099 --> 00:07:56.819
There were a lot of tentpole movies this year…
but did any of them really blow you away?

00:07:56.819 --> 00:08:01.729
In this case, I have to agree with the author
of the article, 2018 is a step down overall

00:08:01.729 --> 00:08:02.940
from previous years.

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:08.330
But, before we wrap this up, let’s revisit
my hypothesis, that 2018 is weaker than recent

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:11.680
years, but that’s partially because recent
years have been pretty strong.

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:15.600
I started this comparison at 2014 because
that's when I started logging my movies.

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:20.930
However, a few Google searches for horror
movies from 2010-2013 brought me back to the

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:22.000
dark ages.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:26.810
An era where you were lucky to get an awesome
horror movie once a year, let alone two, or

00:08:26.810 --> 00:08:27.810
six.

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:30.550
The tide started to turn -- no not that Tide.

00:08:30.550 --> 00:08:34.909
The tide started to turn late in 2014, when
people started to chatter about an incredibly

00:08:34.909 --> 00:08:38.090
clever monster film with creepy synth soundtrack.

00:08:38.090 --> 00:08:41.900
From that point, we would get a new gem every
year, sometimes several.

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:46.380
Twenty-seventeen didn’t have a 5-star mind-blowing
movie leading the way like the previous years,

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:52.300
but it did feature a massive $700M grossing
picture that floated all the way to the top

00:08:52.300 --> 00:08:57.230
of the world of horror, but maybe more impressively
was a hit with fans as well.

00:08:57.230 --> 00:09:02.529
So in my opinion, 2018 is a bit of a dip from
recent years, but I’d still consider it

00:09:02.529 --> 00:09:06.660
be part of a pretty awesome stretch of years
in latter half of this decade.

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:08.610
And we’ve got one more.

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:13.740
In less than 2 months, assuming these wildfires
don’t do to me what Carrie White did to

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:19.420
her prom classmates, I’ll make it to the
long awaited California 2019.

00:09:19.420 --> 00:09:24.740
And it’s sure to be a year for all you crash
queens and motor babies.

00:09:24.740 --> 00:09:26.550
Listen up!

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:27.960
The future is bulletproof!

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:28.920
The Bye Bye Man is secondary.

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:31.480
It’s time to do it now and do it loud.

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:32.600
Horror bois.

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:33.800
Make some noise!

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:37.620
It Chapter 2!

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:38.820
Happy Death Day 2U!

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:40.160
Some mutants that are new.

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:41.260
World War Z.

00:09:41.260 --> 00:09:42.020
Number 2.

00:09:42.020 --> 00:09:42.720
Got a new.

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:43.220
Alien.

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:44.720
Got a werewolf in London.

00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:46.740
Give me more give me more give me more!

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:48.460
You don’t want.

00:09:48.460 --> 00:09:49.300
To be buried.

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:50.300
In a Pet Sematary.

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:52.020
Got some J-horror with Grudge!

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:53.340
No one knows what’s up with Us.

00:09:53.380 --> 00:09:54.100
Child’s Play’s.

00:09:54.140 --> 00:09:54.960
Coming back.

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:56.340
Start the year off right with Glass!

00:09:56.340 --> 00:09:59.460
Give me more, give me more, give me more -- shut
up and sing it with me.

00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:01.160
Na na na na na na.

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:02.480
There’s a new Zombieland.

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:03.980
Na na na na na na.

00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:05.440
Rob Zombie’s coming back.

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:07.000
Na na na na na na.

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:08.280
And we’ll be Conjuring!

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:09.360
Are you a.

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:11.020
Fraid of the dark?

00:10:11.060 --> 00:10:12.380
I hope not.

00:10:12.440 --> 00:10:13.860
Or you will not.

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:15.060
Have a good year.

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:22.200
When 2019 is here….

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:27.280
See you in the next one.

